1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bg5 Bd7 7. Qd2 Rc8 8. O-O-O Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Qa5 10. f4 h6 (10... e6 11. e5 dxe5 12. fxe5 Bc6 13. Bb5 Nd5 14. Bxc6+ bxc6 15. a3 h6 16. Bd2 Qb6 $11 {Karpov-Balashov, USSR Ch 1971}) (10... Rxc3 $1 11. bxc3 e5 12. Qb4 Qxb4 13. cxb4 Nxe4 $11) 11. Bh4 g5 12. e5 (12. Be1 Bg7 13. g3 Bc6 14. Bh3 O-O $5 $13 {R.Byrne-Mestrovic, Hastings 1970/71}) 12... gxh4 13. exf6 e6 14. Be2 Bc6 15. Rhe1 Rg8 16. Bf3 Kd7 17. Re5 Qb6 18. Qxb6 axb6 19. Bh5 Rxg2 20. Bxf7 Rxh2 21. Bxe6+ Kc7 22. Re3 Rd8 23. Nd5+ Bxd5 24. Rxd5 Rf2 25. f5 h5 26. Rc3+ Kb8 27. a4 Rf4 28. Ra3 ({Karpov & Roshal's book give the move order} 28. a5 bxa5 29. Rxa5 Rg4 ({The freeing and equalising move} 29... d5 $1 {goes unmentioned in the Karpov/Roshal book}) 30. Rca3 Kc7 31. Rb5 Bh6+ ({"Black's b-pawn is in danger, and cannot be defended. His one means of counter-play is the continuation} 31... Rg1+ 32. Kd2 Bh6+ {. Byrne, however, begins with the second check, and allows the white king to calmly hide at a2." (Karpov/Roshal annotation)}) 32. Kb1 {and continues as per the text.}) 28... Rg4 29. a5 Bh6+ 30. Kb1 bxa5 31. Rdxa5 Kc7 32. Rb5 Rg3 33. Ra7 Rb8 34. Bd5 Rg1+ 35. Ka2 Rf1 36. Raxb7+ Rxb7 37. Rxb7+ Kd8 38. Be6 h3 39. Rd7+ Ke8 40. Rc7 {Sources: BCM, February 1972, p44; The Chess Player, 1972/2, p2; Informator 13/429 (Parma); Chess is My Life by Anatoly Karpov & Aleksandr Roshal (Pergamon 1980), ppn 65-67} 1-0

