1. e4 g6 2. d4 d6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. f4 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. Be3 Bxf3 7. gxf3 d5 $1 8. exd5 $3 {Undoubtedly one of the finest moves of the tournament. The grandmaster had correctly reasoned that the isolating of the doubled pawns was his best prospect. White’s bishops gained more scope as a result of 8 exd5 (for example, his e4 square could be a valuable post), and any later attempt by Black to increase his activity would be likely to do the same for the white pieces. The objectivity and logic of Unzicker’s thinking appealed to me.} (8. Nxd5 {would be met by} e6 {followed by capture of the d4-pawn.}) (8. Ne2 {would be passive.}) (8. e5 {would leave White with an inferior and static pawn formation.}) 8... Nb4 9. Bb5+ Kf8 10. a3 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. Qe2 Nh6 13. c3 Nf5 14. Bc4 Qd6 15. Bd2 Bf6 16. O-O-O Kg7 17. Bd3 e6 18. Kb1 Rhe8 19. Rhe1 Qd7 {Source: BCM, February 1972, p48} 1/2-1/2

