1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 ({A passive move; preferable is the more direct} 9... Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Be6 {- Golombek}) 10. Bb3 Rc8 $6 (10... Qa5 11. O-O-O Rfc8 12. h4 Ne5 13. Kb1 Nc4 14. Bxc4 Rxc4 15. Nb3 Qc7 $5 $14 {Ciocaltea} (15... Qd8 $5 $16) (15... Qa6 $6 $16)) 11. O-O-O (11. h4 Ne5 12. h5 Nxh5 13. O-O-O Nc4 14. Bxc4 Rxc4 15. g4 Nf6 16. Bh6 $11 {Geller-Korchnoi, Moscow Match Game 4, 1971}) 11... Ne5 (11... a5 $5 {(Gufeld)} 12. h4 Ne5 13. h5 a4 14. Nxa4 Bxa4 15. Bxa4 Nc4 16. Qd3 Qa5 $13 {Ciocaltea}) 12. h4 $1 (12. Bh6 $2 Bxh6 13. Qxh6 Rxc3 $1 $11 {Geller-Korchnoi, Moscow Match Game 2, 1971}) 12... Nc4 (12... h5 $6 13. Bg5 Nh7 14. Bh6 Bxh6 15. Qxh6 Rxc3 $16 {Biyiasis-Kavalek, Vancouver 1971}) 13. Bxc4 Rxc4 14. g4 $1 b5 $1 ({"A critical decision, which this game goes some way to disprove. White cannot afford to take this pawn as Black gets much too much play on the open files but the move doesn't achieve much if it is ignored and I think the older way of playing viz.} 14... Qa5 {with later threat of Rxc3 is better." (Alexander; he wrote that his comments were based on those after the game by Byrne)}) 15. h5 b4 16. Nd5 $1 {"As Byrne says, Black has taken two moves forcing the knight to go where it was going anyway." (Alexander)} Nxd5 17. exd5 Qa5 18. Kb1 Rfc8 (18... Qxd5 $2 19. Nf5 $1 Bxf5 (19... Qxd2 20. Nxe7+) 20. Qxd5 Rxc2 21. gxf5 Rxb2+ 22. Ka1 $1 $18 {with the idea 23 Bd4 - Ciocaltea}) 19. hxg6 fxg6 ({Still worse would be} 19... hxg6 {since then after} 20. Qh2 {White threatens Qh7+ followed by Bh6 - Golombek}) 20. Qh2 $1 $16 (20. Nb3 $6 Qc7 21. Rh2 a5 $1 $13 {Ciocaltea}) 20... h6 $6 (20... Kf7 $5 $14 {Korchnoi}) (20... h5 $6 21. gxh5 $18 {Ciocaltea}) 21. Rd2 {With the idea 22 Nb3, 23 Bxh6 - Ciocaltea} ({So that if now} 21. Bxh6 Rxd4 22. Bxg7 Rxd1+ 23. Rxd1 Kxg7 {and Black has emerged from the mating attack - Golombek}) 21... Qxd5 {Seemingly emerging from his difficulties since now his queen is ready to go to the aid of his king; but now comes a fine stroke - Golombek} 22. Nf5 $1 Qe6 (22... Qf7 23. Bxh6 gxf5 24. Bxg7 Qg6 (24... Qxg7 25. gxf5 $18) 25. gxf5 $1 Bxf5 26. Bc3 $3 $18 {Ciocaltea}) 23. Bxh6 gxf5 24. Bxg7 Kxg7 25. Re2 $1 $18 {Very strong; nothing much is attained by the immediate check - Golombek} Qf6 ({If now} 25... Qf7 26. Qh6+ Kg8 27. Qh8#) 26. g5 $1 (26. Qh7+ $1 Kf8 27. g5 $1 $18) 26... Qxg5 27. Rg2 Rg4 28. fxg4 Bc6 29. Qh7+ Kf8 30. Rh5 Qe3 31. Qxf5+ {Sources: The Chess Player, 1972/2, p16 (Ciocaltea); Informator; The Times, 15 January 1972 (Golombek); Sunday Times, 20 February 1972 (CHO'D Alexander)} 1-0

