1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e6 7. O-O Be7 8. f4 O-O (8... Qc7 9. Be3 b5 $2 10. Bf3 {Minic-Maric, Informator 5/446}) 9. Be3 (9. Kh1 $5 {Korchnoi}) 9... Qc7 10. Qe1 (10. Bf3 {Korchnoi}) (10. g4 {Korchnoi}) 10... b5 11. Bf3 Bb7 12. e5 ({The advanced pawn that results on e5 after this move is more of a liability than a help; preferable seems} 12. a3 {- Golombek}) 12... dxe5 13. fxe5 Nfd7 14. Bxb7 $2 (14. Qg3 $1 Bxf3 (14... Bc5 $1 15. Ne4 $1 Bxd4 16. Bxd4 Nc6 17. c3 $14 {Korchnoi}) 15. Nxf3 Bc5 $14 {Stein-Spassky, USSR 1964}) 14... Qxb7 15. Qg3 Bc5 $1 16. Rad1 Nc6 (16... Qc7 $2 17. Ne4 $16 {Minic-Maric}) 17. Nxc6 (17. Bh6 g6 18. Bxf8 Bxd4+ 19. Rxd4 Nxd4 $15 {Korchnoi} 20. Bd6 Nf5 $15 {Minic-Maric}) 17... Qxc6 18. Kh1 {Threatening to win the ex­change by Bh6; hence Black hastens to exchange off Bishops - Golombek} (18. Bxc5 Qxc5+ 19. Kh1 (19. Rf2 Nxe5 20. Ne4 Qc7 21. Nf6+ Kh8 22. Nd7 Rfd8 23. Rfd2 Qa7+) 19... Rad8 20. Ne4 Qxc2 $1 $17 {Korchnoi}) 18... Bxe3 19. Qxe3 Qc7 $17 (19... Nb6 $5 20. b3 Rad8 $17 {Korchnoi}) 20. Rfe1 (20. Rd6 f6 $1 {Korchnoi}) 20... b4 {( Minic-Maric)} 21. Ne4 (21. Na4 $5 Rad8 {(with the idea Nxe5)} 22. h3 Qxc2 23. b3 Nb8 24. Rxd8 Rxd8 25. Qb6 Qd2 $44 {Korchnoi}) 21... Nxe5 22. Qg3 f6 ({If} 22... Qb8 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Nxh7 Kxh7 25. Rxe5 {with excellent attacking chances against Black's weakened kingside - Golombek}) 23. Nxf6+ Rxf6 24. Rxe5 ({And not} 24. Qxe5 $4 {on account of} Rf1+ {- Golombek}) 24... Raf8 25. h3 (25. Ree1 $5 {Korchnoi} Qxc2 $17 {Minic-Maric}) 25... Qxc2 26. Qe1 (26. Qb3 $2 Qxb3 27. axb3 Rf1+ 28. Rxf1 Rxf1+ 29. Kh2 Kf7 $19 {Korchnoi}) 26... Rf2 27. Rg5 Kh8 $2 (27... Rf1+ 28. Qxf1 Rxf1+ 29. Rxf1 Qxb2 {( Minic-Maric)} 30. Ra5 $15 {Korchnoi}) (27... e5 $1 28. Kh2 Re2 29. Qg1 Qc7 $19 {Korchnoi}) 28. b3 Re2 ({Stronger seems} 28... h6 {followed by a tripling of pieces on the seventh rank; but already Korchnoi was in acute time trouble and all his moves from now on were played a tempo - Golombek}) 29. Qg1 Qc3 $2 (29... h6 $1 {Korchnoi}) 30. Ra5 Qc6 31. Rc1 Qb7 $6 (31... Qe4 32. Rc4 Qb7 $17 {Korchnoi}) 32. Re1 {(! Minic-Maric)} Rc2 33. Rxe6 h6 34. Rae5 $2 (34. Raxa6 $1 Rff2 35. Rac6 $1 $15 {Korchnoi}) 34... Rff2 35. Re4 Qd5 36. Kh2 Qd2 37. Rg6 Rc1 38. Rd4 Qe3 39. Rd1 Qf4+ 40. Rg3 Rc3 41. Rd8+ Kh7 42. Qb1+ Rfc2 {On adjournment Unzicker, instead of sealing the forced move 43 Qe1 and going away to analyse (and thinking he had no defence to 43...Rxa2) resigned. A surprising decision for a player of his experience, due, no doubt, to the battering he had received. Actually, after 43...Rxa2, he could have mated in four moves starting 44 Rh8+ (Korchnoi had seen this). Fortunately, for Unzicker's peace of mind, analysis revealed several roads to victory for Korchnoi, though none of them simple. (CHESS Magazine) (Sources: The Times, 22 January 1972 (Golombek); The Chess Player, 1972/2, p20 (Korchnoi); Informator 13/482 (Minic-Maric)} (42... Rfc2 43. Qe1 Rf2 $1 (43... Rxa2 $4 44. Rh8+ Kxh8 45. Qe8+ Kh7 46. Qg6+ Kh8 47. Qxg7#) 44. Qb1+ Rcc2 45. Qg1 g5 $1 $19 {Golombek} (45... Rc1 $2 46. Rd7 $1 $11) (45... Rxa2 46. Rd7 Rxg2+ 47. Qxg2 Rxg2+ 48. Kxg2 Qf6 $19 {is the much less convincing line allegedly given by Korchnoi in The Chess Player and Minic-Maric in Informator})) 0-1

